 # ADR-014 — Feedback Hue Separation and Terracotta-Luminance-Matched Ramp Regeneration
 
 **Date:** 2026-07-07
+**Amended:** 2026-10-09
 **Status:** `accepted`
 
 ## Context
@@ -54,6 +55,23 @@ This was applied to cyan's entire light ramp in this pass. It is the documented
 - **Trade-off accepted:** the `tomato`-vs-`red` choice and the whole-ramp cyan regen mean red and cyan no longer share step-by-step hex parity with any prior scale; contrast for the repointed `feedback.error` pairs was re-verified against the built theme CSS rather than assumed to carry over from terracotta.
 - **Known debt tracked, not hidden:** cyan's dark-ramp dip (deferred) and issue #22's ProgressBar-fill waiver remain, each documented above and in the waiver ledger.
 
+## Amendment (2026-10-09) — `green` and `yellow` feedback hues; full feedback token set
+
+The theme layer had lost its `feedback.*` tokens while TextField and Select still referenced `color.text.feedback.error` / `color.border.feedback.error`, so their error state rendered uncoloured. The full set — `color.{background,text,border,icon}.feedback.{error,success,warning}` — is now defined in both themes.
+
+Applying rule (a) to the two new tones: the conventional candidates `teal` (success) and `amber` (warning) are brand slots (`upskill.accent`, `horizon.accent`), so they are ruled out. Two new non-brand-eligible hues were added, sourced verbatim from Radix (`@radix-ui/colors@3.0.0`) like `red`: **`green`** (success) and **`yellow`** (warning). Radix `orange` was not chosen for warning — it reads too close to terracotta and red, the same reason `tomato` was rejected. Like `red`, both hues are reserved for feedback and must never be mapped to a brand slot.
+
+Step mapping (light / dark):
+
+| Role | error | success | warning |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `background` | `red.3` / `red.dark.3` | `green.3` / `green.dark.3` | `yellow.3` / `yellow.dark.3` |
+| `text` | `red.11` / `red.dark.11` | `green.12` / `green.dark.11` | `yellow.12` / `yellow.dark.11` |
+| `border` | `red.11` / `red.dark.11` | `green.11` / `green.dark.11` | `yellow.11` / `yellow.dark.11` |
+| `icon` | `red.11` / `red.dark.11` | `green.11` / `green.dark.11` | `yellow.11` / `yellow.dark.11` |
+
+Light success/warning text uses step 12 because Radix `green.11` (4.21:1) and `yellow.11` (4.27:1) miss 4.5:1 on their own step-3 band, and they still miss it on step 1. This means the verbatim `green.11`/`yellow.11` do not meet the step-11-as-text invariant above. They are kept verbatim and used only for 3:1 roles (border, icon) rather than darkened ad hoc. If either is ever needed as text, regenerate it with method (b). All feedback pairs in `scripts/token-contrast-check.js` pass for every brand and theme with no new waivers.
+
 ## Related
 
 - **ADR-002** (three-layer/now four-layer token model) — governs the primitive hue list; amended the same date to record the addition of `red`, cross-referencing this ADR.
